                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown interviews Alex Kantrowitz of Big Technology to argue that Wall Street is systematically underestimating the long-term compute demand that agentic AI will create. Brown contends that current analyst focus on chatbot use cases misses the exponential infrastructure requirements that will accompany autonomous vehicles, robots, and fully automated systems. The piece frames ongoing hyperscaler capex not as speculative overbuilding but as rational, decade-horizon bets on inevitable AI infrastructure demand.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown is making the most important bull case for AI infrastructure that most equity analysts are not pricing: the gap between current compute supply and the eventual demand from billions of AI agents running continuously is not a rounding error, it is a multi-trillion-dollar structural shortage. If he and Kantrowitz are right, the market\u0027s periodic hand-wringing over hyperscaler capex efficiency is a category errorÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âit is judging a long-duration infrastructure bet by short-duration ROI metrics. Investors with a 5-10 year horizon should treat every AI capex pullback fear as a potential entry point into the picks-and-shovels layer.",
