                                                              "summary":  "Josh Brown profiles Ron Baron and his son Michael, exploring how the firm grew from $10 million to roughly $70 billion in AUM through early, concentrated bets on Tesla and SpaceX when Wall Street consensus was dismissive. The piece highlights Baron\u0027s conviction-driven, hold-through-volatility philosophy and frames his track record as the best real-world proof that long-duration, high-conviction growth investing still works in modern markets.",
                                                              "take":  "The Baron story is worth internalizing not as a feel-good narrative but as a practical decision framework: the alpha in his track record came almost entirely from not selling, not from better stock-picking. That is an underrated insight in an era of constant portfolio rebalancing and factor rotation. The uncomfortable implication for most investors is that their processÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âbuilt around risk management, position limits, and rebalancingÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Âis structurally incompatible with generating Baron-style returns. If you want his outcomes you have to accept his stomach-churning holding periods, which most institutional mandates explicitly prohibit.",
